1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:04,666
<font color="#000000">This paper, from KU Leuven and presented at ICML 2023,</font>

2
00:00:04,746 --> 00:00:08,912
<font color="#000000">revisits Kernel Principal Component Analysis
through the lens of convex duality.</font>

3
00:00:08,992 --> 00:00:13,158
<font color="#000000">The authors reformulate Kernel PCA as a difference
of convex functions problem,</font>

4
00:00:13,238 --> 00:00:17,404
<font color="#000000">then dualize it.</font>

5
00:00:17,484 --> 00:00:21,650
<font color="#000000">This single move unlocks three things at once:
gradient-based solvers that avoid the expensive</font>

6
00:00:21,730 --> 00:00:25,896
<font color="#000000">cubic singular value decomposition of the Gram matrix,</font>

7
00:00:25,976 --> 00:00:30,142
<font color="#000000">a flexible framework that swaps in robust or sparse
objectives through Moreau envelopes,</font>

8
00:00:30,222 --> 00:00:34,388
<font color="#000000">and significant speedups on real benchmarks.</font>

9
00:00:34,468 --> 00:00:38,634
<font color="#000000">It is a clean example of how the right optimization
viewpoint can extend a classical method</font>

10
00:00:38,714 --> 00:00:42,880
<font color="#000000">in several directions simultaneously.</font>

11
00:00:43,260 --> 00:00:47,622
<font color="#000000">Kernel PCA is one of the most widely used tools in
unsupervised learning,</font>

12
00:00:47,703 --> 00:00:52,065
<font color="#000000">but it has a stubborn scaling problem.</font>

13
00:00:52,145 --> 00:00:56,508
<font color="#000000">The standard recipe computes the singular value
decomposition of the n-by-n Gram matrix,</font>

14
00:00:56,588 --> 00:01:00,950
<font color="#000000">which costs order n-cubed and becomes painfully
slow even for moderately sized datasets.</font>

15
00:01:01,030 --> 00:01:05,393
<font color="#000000">On top of that,</font>

16
00:01:05,473 --> 00:01:09,835
<font color="#000000">every time researchers wanted a robust or a sparse
variant of Kernel PCA,</font>

17
00:01:09,915 --> 00:01:14,278
<font color="#000000">they reached for a different ad-hoc formulation or
weighting heuristic,</font>

18
00:01:14,358 --> 00:01:18,720
<font color="#000000">producing a scattered collection of unrelated
optimization problems rather than one coherent method.</font>

19
00:01:19,100 --> 00:01:23,671
<font color="#000000">The authors observe that Kernel PCA can be written
as variance maximization under orthonormality constraints,</font>

20
00:01:23,751 --> 00:01:28,322
<font color="#000000">which puts it squarely in the family of
difference-of-convex problems that optimization researchers understand well.</font>

21
00:01:28,402 --> 00:01:32,973
<font color="#000000">Earlier work had studied PCA as a
difference-of-convex program,</font>

22
00:01:33,053 --> 00:01:37,624
<font color="#000000">but only for linear PCA,</font>

23
00:01:37,704 --> 00:01:42,276
<font color="#000000">and often only for the first component where the
orthogonality constraints vanish.</font>

24
00:01:42,356 --> 00:01:46,927
<font color="#000000">Meanwhile,</font>

25
00:01:47,007 --> 00:01:51,578
<font color="#000000">a separate line of research had shown that infimal
convolution is an elegant way to build robust or sparse losses,</font>

26
00:01:51,658 --> 00:01:56,229
<font color="#000000">and that these constructions behave especially
nicely in dual formulations.</font>

27
00:01:56,309 --> 00:02:00,880
<font color="#000000">Bringing these two observations together is the
opening the paper exploits.</font>

28
00:02:01,260 --> 00:02:05,202
<font color="#000000">The core contribution is a general dual-based
formulation of Kernel PCA built on the dualization</font>

29
00:02:05,282 --> 00:02:09,223
<font color="#000000">of a difference of convex functions.</font>

30
00:02:09,303 --> 00:02:13,245
<font color="#000000">This formulation does two things at once.</font>

31
00:02:13,325 --> 00:02:17,267
<font color="#000000">First, it turns the problem into one over a
finite-dimensional dual matrix,</font>

32
00:02:17,347 --> 00:02:21,288
<font color="#000000">which can be solved with efficient gradient-based
methods and avoids the expensive singular value decomposition,</font>

33
00:02:21,368 --> 00:02:25,310
<font color="#000000">even when the feature space is infinite-dimensional.</font>

34
00:02:25,390 --> 00:02:29,332
<font color="#000000">Second,</font>

35
00:02:29,412 --> 00:02:33,353
<font color="#000000">it makes the objective modular: by choosing
objectives expressible as Moreau envelopes,</font>

36
00:02:33,433 --> 00:02:37,375
<font color="#000000">the same framework promotes robustness or sparsity
without leaving the dual picture.</font>

37
00:02:37,455 --> 00:02:41,397
<font color="#000000">The authors supply the supporting theory,</font>

38
00:02:41,477 --> 00:02:45,418
<font color="#000000">concrete L-BFGS and difference-of-convex algorithms,</font>

39
00:02:45,498 --> 00:02:49,440
<font color="#000000">and the proximal operators needed to make the
robust and sparse cases practical.</font>

40
00:02:49,820 --> 00:02:55,539
<font color="#FFFFFF">The method starts by writing Kernel PCA as the
minimization of a difference of two convex</font>

41
00:02:55,619 --> 00:02:59,318
<font color="#FFFFFF">functions over the Stiefel manifold of orthonormal frames.</font>

42
00:02:59,398 --> 00:03:03,748
<font color="#FFFFFF">A general dualization proposition then converts
this primal problem,</font>

43
00:03:03,828 --> 00:03:08,309
<font color="#FFFFFF">whose variables live in a possibly
infinite-dimensional feature space,</font>

44
00:03:08,389 --> 00:03:12,414
<font color="#FFFFFF">into a dual problem whose variable H is a finite
n-by-s matrix,</font>

45
00:03:12,494 --> 00:03:14,824
<font color="#FFFFFF">perfectly suited to gradient descent.</font>

46
00:03:14,904 --> 00:03:19,124
<font color="#FFFFFF">The indicator function of the Stiefel manifold
turns, in the dual,</font>

47
00:03:19,204 --> 00:03:22,382
<font color="#FFFFFF">into a nuclear-norm term the authors call pi of H,</font>

48
00:03:22,462 --> 00:03:26,096
<font color="#FFFFFF">equal to the trace of the square root of H-transpose G H.</font>

49
00:03:26,176 --> 00:03:31,634
<font color="#FFFFFF">Its gradient only requires the singular value
decomposition of a small s-by-s matrix,</font>

50
00:03:31,714 --> 00:03:36,064
<font color="#FFFFFF">not the full n-by-n Gram matrix, which is the
source of the speedup.</font>

51
00:03:36,144 --> 00:03:38,736
<font color="#FFFFFF">For the standard problem they run L-BFGS.</font>

52
00:03:38,816 --> 00:03:40,951
<font color="#FFFFFF">To promote robustness or sparsity,</font>

53
00:03:41,031 --> 00:03:47,466
<font color="#FFFFFF">they add a Moreau-envelope term to the objective
and optimize with a difference-of-convex algorithm,</font>

54
00:03:47,546 --> 00:03:53,200
<font color="#FFFFFF">plugging in closed-form proximal operators for the
Huber and epsilon-insensitive losses.</font>

55
00:03:53,580 --> 00:03:57,026
<font color="#000000">The experiments span synthetic and real-world data.</font>

56
00:03:57,106 --> 00:04:02,488
<font color="#000000">For efficiency, the authors time Kernel PCA on a
synthetic 7,000-point problem,</font>

57
00:04:02,568 --> 00:04:05,600
<font color="#000000">the Protein dataset with about 15,000 points,</font>

58
00:04:05,680 --> 00:04:11,476
<font color="#000000">the RCV1 text collection with roughly 20,000
points, and CIFAR-10 with 60,000 images.</font>

59
00:04:11,556 --> 00:04:18,805
<font color="#000000">Robustness is tested by artificially contaminating
the Iris dataset with multiplicative Gaussian outliers,</font>

60
00:04:18,885 --> 00:04:24,820
<font color="#000000">and sparsity is measured through reconstruction
error under epsilon-insensitive losses.</font>

61
00:04:24,900 --> 00:04:31,043
<font color="#000000">All solvers are compared fairly using a shared
relative dual-cost residual as the stopping</font>

62
00:04:31,123 --> 00:04:33,394
<font color="#000000">criterion at two tolerance levels,</font>

63
00:04:33,474 --> 00:04:36,920
<font color="#000000">and the timing results are averaged over five runs.</font>

64
00:04:37,300 --> 00:04:42,077
<font color="#000000">On the efficiency benchmarks, the proposed solver
wins across the board.</font>

65
00:04:42,157 --> 00:04:47,811
<font color="#000000">At the looser tolerance of ten-to-the-minus-two, it
is faster than full SVD, Lanczos,</font>

66
00:04:47,891 --> 00:04:50,038
<font color="#000000">and randomized SVD on every task,</font>

67
00:04:50,118 --> 00:04:53,950
<font color="#000000">and it is at least three times faster than randomized SVD,</font>

68
00:04:54,030 --> 00:05:00,089
<font color="#000000">peaking at a nine-point-one-seven times speedup on
CIFAR-10 with its sixty thousand images,</font>

69
00:05:00,169 --> 00:05:04,070
<font color="#000000">where full SVD could not even finish within thirty
minutes.</font>

70
00:05:04,150 --> 00:05:05,419
<font color="#000000">Just as importantly,</font>

71
00:05:05,499 --> 00:05:11,355
<font color="#000000">the method is robust to a property that hurts
randomized SVD badly: as the eigenspectrum</font>

72
00:05:11,435 --> 00:05:13,919
<font color="#000000">of the Gram matrix decays more slowly,</font>

73
00:05:13,999 --> 00:05:16,820
<font color="#000000">randomized SVD needs many more oversamples,</font>

74
00:05:16,900 --> 00:05:20,800
<font color="#000000">while the proposed solver's iteration count barely
changes.</font>

75
00:05:21,180 --> 00:05:24,191
<font color="#000000">Several controlled studies dissect the behavior.</font>

76
00:05:24,271 --> 00:05:29,986
<font color="#000000">Sweeping the Huber loss parameter kappa shows that
both Huber variants consistently reduce</font>

77
00:05:30,066 --> 00:05:35,589
<font color="#000000">mean squared error on the contaminated Iris data
compared to the ordinary squared loss,</font>

78
00:05:35,669 --> 00:05:39,581
<font color="#000000">confirming the robustness the framework is designed
to induce.</font>

79
00:05:39,661 --> 00:05:45,183
<font color="#000000">Varying the number of components shows that the
L-BFGS solver keeps its speed advantage</font>

80
00:05:45,263 --> 00:05:47,759
<font color="#000000">over randomized SVD across a wide range,</font>

81
00:05:47,839 --> 00:05:54,198
<font color="#000000">and only at very large numbers of components does
the per-iteration small SVD start to slow it down.</font>

82
00:05:54,278 --> 00:06:00,380
<font color="#000000">And the eigenspectrum study pinpoints the scaling
advantage: as the spectrum decays more slowly,</font>

83
00:06:00,460 --> 00:06:07,850
<font color="#000000">randomized SVD's oversample count climbs sharply
while the proposed solver's iteration count stays essentially flat.</font>

84
00:06:08,230 --> 00:06:10,332
<font color="#000000">A few numbers capture the impact.</font>

85
00:06:10,412 --> 00:06:16,218
<font color="#000000">On CIFAR-10 the method delivers a
nine-point-one-seven times speedup over randomized SVD,</font>

86
00:06:16,298 --> 00:06:21,707
<font color="#000000">and it is at least three times faster than
randomized SVD on every efficiency task.</font>

87
00:06:21,787 --> 00:06:29,444
<font color="#000000">The key complexity change is that each iteration
costs order s-cubed for a small s-by-s singular value decomposition,</font>

88
00:06:29,524 --> 00:06:34,470
<font color="#000000">replacing the classical order n-cubed decomposition
of the full Gram matrix.</font>

89
00:06:34,550 --> 00:06:35,991
<font color="#000000">On the robustness side,</font>

90
00:06:36,071 --> 00:06:42,339
<font color="#000000">the Huber loss cuts the mean squared error on
contaminated Iris from about seven-point-five-nine</font>

91
00:06:42,419 --> 00:06:46,373
<font color="#000000">down to six-point-eight-three at the tested
corruption level.</font>

92
00:06:46,453 --> 00:06:50,539
<font color="#000000">And these results scale to datasets with sixty
thousand points,</font>

93
00:06:50,619 --> 00:06:54,110
<font color="#000000">where full SVD could not finish within thirty minutes.</font>

94
00:06:54,490 --> 00:07:00,108
<font color="#000000">The lasting message is that a single change of
viewpoint pays off in three directions at once.</font>

95
00:07:00,188 --> 00:07:05,018
<font color="#000000">By writing Kernel PCA as a difference of convex
functions and moving to the dual,</font>

96
00:07:05,098 --> 00:07:10,352
<font color="#000000">the authors get a solver that avoids the expensive
singular value decomposition and runs</font>

97
00:07:10,432 --> 00:07:13,019
<font color="#000000">up to nine times faster than randomized SVD,</font>

98
00:07:13,099 --> 00:07:18,293
<font color="#000000">and in the same breath they gain a modular way to
demand robustness or sparsity just by</font>

99
00:07:18,373 --> 00:07:20,960
<font color="#000000">picking the right Moreau-envelope objective.</font>

100
00:07:21,040 --> 00:07:24,294
<font color="#000000">Because the dual variable is always finite-dimensional,</font>

101
00:07:24,374 --> 00:07:30,840
<font color="#000000">the framework even reaches infinite-dimensional
feature maps that classical Kernel PCA simply cannot handle.</font>
